1
00:02:35,172 --> 00:02:41,172
Awaken to a World of Wonders!

2
00:05:10,344 --> 00:05:16,344
Sleeping Beauty is a fantasy, animation, romance
film directed by Clyde Geronimi.

3
00:07:45,517 --> 00:07:51,517
Cursed to die by the evil fairy Maleficent when she was a baby, Princess Aurora
is sent into hiding under protection from three good fairies.

4
00:10:20,689 --> 00:10:26,689
As she grows up far away, Maleficent becomes increasingly
determined to seal the princess's fate.

5
00:12:55,862 --> 00:13:01,862
Princess Aurora (voice), played by Mary Costa.

6
00:15:31,034 --> 00:15:37,034
Prince Phillip (voice), played by Bill Shirley.

7
00:18:06,206 --> 00:18:12,206
Maleficent (voice), played by Eleanor Audley.

8
00:20:41,379 --> 00:20:47,379
Flora / Queen Leah (voice), played by Verna Felton.

9
00:23:16,551 --> 00:23:22,551
Merryweather (voice), played by Barbara Luddy.

10
00:25:51,724 --> 00:25:57,724
Fauna (voice), played by Barbara Jo Allen.

11
00:28:26,896 --> 00:28:32,896
Sleeping Beauty is a 1959 American animated musical fantasy film produced by Walt
Disney Productions and released by Buena Vista Film Distribution.

12
00:31:02,068 --> 00:31:08,068
Based on Charles Perrault's 1697 fairy tale, the film follows Princess Aurora, who was cursed by the evil fairy
Maleficent to die from pricking her finger on the spindle of a spinning wheel on her 16th birthday.

13
00:33:37,241 --> 00:33:43,241
She is saved by three good fairies, who alter Aurora's curse so that she falls
into a deep sleep and will be awakened by true love's kiss.

14
00:36:12,413 --> 00:36:18,413
The production was supervised by Clyde Geronimi, and was directed
by Wolfgang Reitherman, Eric Larson, and Les Clark.

15
00:38:47,586 --> 00:38:53,586
It features the voices of Mary Costa, Bill Shirley, Eleanor Audley, Verna Felton,
Barbara Luddy, Barbara Jo Allen, Taylor Holmes, and Bill Thompson.

16
00:41:22,758 --> 00:41:28,758
Sleeping Beauty began development in 1950.

17
00:43:57,931 --> 00:44:03,931
The film took nearly a decade and $6 million (equivalent to $64,719,178 in 2024) to
produce, and was Disney's most expensive animated feature at the time.

18
00:46:33,103 --> 00:46:39,103
Its tapestry-like art style was devised by Eyvind Earle, who was inspired by pre-Renaissance European art;
its score and songs, composed by George Bruns, were based on Pyotr Tchaikovsky's 1889 ballet.

19
00:49:08,275 --> 00:49:14,275
Sleeping Beauty was the first animated film to use the Super Technirama 70 widescreen process and was the second
full-length animated feature filmed in anamorphic widescreen, following Lady and the Tramp (1955).

20
00:51:43,448 --> 00:51:49,448
It was released in theaters on January 29, 1959, to mixed reviews from critics who praised
its art direction and musical score, but criticized its plot and characters.

21
00:54:18,620 --> 00:54:24,620
The film was a box-office bomb in its initial release, grossing $5.3 million (equivalent to $57,168,607
in 2024), and losing $900,000 (equivalent to $9,707,877 in 2024) for the distributor.

22
00:56:53,793 --> 00:56:59,793
Many employees from the animation studio were laid off.

23
00:59:28,965 --> 00:59:34,965
Sleeping Beauty's re-releases have been successful, and it has become
one of Disney's most artistically acclaimed features.

24
01:02:04,137 --> 01:02:10,137
The film was nominated for the Academy Award for Best Scoring of
a Musical Picture at the 32nd Academy Awards.

25
01:04:39,310 --> 01:04:45,310
Maleficent, a live-action reimagining of the film from Maleficent's perspective, was
released in 2014, followed by a sequel, Maleficent: Mistress of Evil, in 2019.

26
01:07:14,482 --> 01:07:20,482
The latter year, Sleeping Beauty was selected for preservation in the United States Library of
Congress' National Film Registry as "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

27
01:09:49,655 --> 01:09:55,655
The film explores themes of witch, princess, magic, kingdom, fairy tale.

28
01:12:24,827 --> 01:12:30,827
The tone is emotional.
